WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:05.840 align:start position:19%
If you've been adding weight to the bar
each session, but your lifts have

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:10.560 align:start position:19%
started to stall, you need to change the
way that you push for progress. You need

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:14.760 align:start position:19%
a system, and today I'm going to
introduce you to a really good one.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.800 align:start position:19%
In this video, I'll explain something
called auto-regulated double

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.640 align:start position:19%
progression.
This is from the hypertrophy progression

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.480 align:start position:19%
system in our best-selling [music]
muscle and strength pyramid training

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.480 align:start position:19%
book.
Now, over 100,000 of you have kindly

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:30.280 align:start position:19%
purchased the book over the years, so I
figure it's about time that I made a

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.080 align:start position:19%
video on it.
Now, auto-regulated double progression,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.760 align:start position:19%
that may sound complicated, but it's
actually quite simple.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:42.000 align:start position:19%
The double progression part simply means
that instead of trying to progress by

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:46.200 align:start position:19%
lifting more weight each session, we aim
to do more reps so that we can get

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:50.920 align:start position:19%
stronger before then dropping that down
and increasing the load.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:54.000 align:start position:19%
The auto-regulation part, in this case,
means we have a rule for how close to

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:58.040 align:start position:19%
failure that we train.
So, we lighten loads on days that we're

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:02.360 align:start position:19%
feeling fatigued, and we increase them
on our high energy days.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:06.240 align:start position:19%
This is in contrast to a pre-planned
program demanding strict adherence

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:11.600 align:start position:19%
regardless of how you feel.
The idea is that it helps us avoid

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:14.440 align:start position:19%
plateaus by avoiding burnout and injury.
When we put these two things together,

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.320 align:start position:19%
along with some rules for lighter weeks
called deloads to avoid fatigue building

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:22.560 align:start position:19%
too high, we have a powerful system for
driving progress that will serve you for

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:26.920 align:start position:19%
years to come. But, before I get to
that, allow me to explain linear

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:30.390 align:start position:19%
progression because this is what
auto-regulated double progression builds

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.040 align:start position:19%
on.
&gt;&gt; [music]

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.280 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Now, linear progression is simply means
to increase the load by a fixed amount

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.880 align:start position:19%
each session. It's probably what you did
naturally when you first went to the

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:42.160 align:start position:19%
gym.
Now, this is the case if you're

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:45.760 align:start position:19%
relatively new to lifting, new to an
exercise, or returning to training after

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:50.240 align:start position:19%
some time off.
Now, I recommend using a fixed amount,

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:54.320 align:start position:19%
not increasing by as much as you can
each session because that will be better

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:58.400 align:start position:19%
for your motor learning, and also your
connective tissue development, helping

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:02.000 align:start position:19%
you avoid injury. Now, in our programs,
which are all free, by the way, the

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.761 align:start position:19%
links is in the description, you'll see
that every lift has a notation next to

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.679 align:start position:19%
it that looks something like this: 3
[music] by 15.

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:13.600 align:start position:19%
Now, the first number is the number of
sets that you'll perform. The second

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:18.240 align:start position:19%
number is the number of reps that you'll
target in each set. Now, this may as be

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:22.760 align:start position:19%
a single number, like 15, or it may be a
range, like 10 to [music] 15.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:26.760 align:start position:19%
Now, for your first session, perform a
few warm-up sets, and then choose a load

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:29.996 align:start position:19%
that you're confident that you'll feel
comfortable doing all of the sets and

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:32.160 align:start position:19%
reps listed.
&gt;&gt; [music]

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.680 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; So, if a rep range is given, this means
the highest number in that range.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.680 align:start position:19%
Now, to help you get used to a new
routine and minimize unnecessary fatigue

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:45.680 align:start position:19%
and soreness, consider doing one set
less than prescribed for all exercises

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:50.720 align:start position:19%
in the first week. So, for example,
if your program says to do three sets of

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:56.440 align:start position:19%
10 to 15, and you think that you can
lift 40 lb for three sets of 15,

00:02:53.880 --> 00:03:02.200 align:start position:19%
but you're confident that you can do 30
lb for three sets of 15, start with 30

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:05.360 align:start position:19%
lb and do two sets of 15. Yes, that's
going to feel super easy, but that's

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.760 align:start position:19%
what we want, and we're going to build
up slowly.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.440 align:start position:19%
Then, in the second week, you're going
to perform three sets with the same

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.360 align:start position:19%
load, and in the third week, if you
could do that with good form, then

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.640 align:start position:19%
you're going to increase the load again
and continue to do that wherever

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.400 align:start position:19%
possible.
Here's a table of how linear progression

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:25.280 align:start position:19%
might look for three sets of 15 reps
with 5 lb as the smallest increment.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:28.400 align:start position:19%
Feel free to pause the video now to
review the table for longer or to take a

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:30.959 align:start position:19%
screenshot so that you can reference it
in the gym.

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:34.959 align:start position:19%
Once you're no longer able to progress
like this, it's time to use the

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:39.120 align:start position:19%
auto-regulated double progression
system.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.280 align:start position:19%
Now, in this system, our goal is to
increase the number of reps that we can

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:47.000 align:start position:19%
get each session until we are strong
enough to then increase the load. This

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:50.280 align:start position:19%
is the double progression part.
The auto-regulation part adds another

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:55.600 align:start position:19%
rule for how close to failure that we
train. This is called RIR, or reps in

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:59.640 align:start position:19%
reserve, and it indicates how many reps
short of failure we should train.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:03.960 align:start position:19%
So, in our programs, you're going to see
RIR noted next to each exercise after

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:07.480 align:start position:19%
the @ symbol.
It might be a single number, or it might

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:11.880 align:start position:19%
be the range.
A three RIR means you could do three

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:16.760 align:start position:19%
more reps. A two RIR means you could do
two more reps. A one RIR means you could

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:21.079 align:start position:19%
do one more rep, and a zero RIR means
you can complete that rep, but not do

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:24.440 align:start position:19%
any more full reps.
Now, for auto-regulated double

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:30.840 align:start position:19%
progression, when you can hit the top
end of the rep range target for all sets

00:04:27.919 --> 00:04:35.120 align:start position:19%
at the highest end of the RIR range in
your first set, then you increase the

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:37.640 align:start position:19%
load in the next session. This is
easiest if I explain with an example.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:41.880 align:start position:19%
So,
if the exercise has

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:48.203 align:start position:19%
three sets of 10 to 15
at a two to zero RIR listed, then your

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:50.680 align:start position:19%
goal is to get 15 reps for all sets,
&gt;&gt; [music]

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:54.520 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; and have the RIR for your first set no
higher than two.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:56.160 align:start position:19%
So, here's how things might look for a
novice lifter.

00:04:55.408 --> 00:05:00.440 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; [music]
&gt;&gt; In session one, you can lift 40 lb for

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:03.800 align:start position:19%
12 reps in your first set when stopping
two reps short of failure.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:08.400 align:start position:19%
And then in your second and third sets,
you get 12 and 11 reps, respectively.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:13.600 align:start position:19%
In session two, you choose the same
load, and you get 14, 14, and 12 reps

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:16.919 align:start position:19%
with that first set again being a two
RIR.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:21.840 align:start position:19%
In session three, you choose the same
load, and you get 15, 14, and 14 reps

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:26.520 align:start position:19%
with the first set being a two RIR. Now,
this means you've achieved your target,

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:31.320 align:start position:19%
so you can increase the load by 5 lb to
45 lb in the fourth session.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:34.919 align:start position:19%
And because this is heavier in that
fourth session,

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:40.680 align:start position:19%
you can only get 11 reps for your first
set while keeping to the two RIR rule.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:43.840 align:start position:19%
Now, in a more advanced lifter, the rate
of progression is obviously going to be

00:05:41.919 --> 00:05:48.480 align:start position:19%
slower, and it will take more sessions
to increase the load while keeping to

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:52.080 align:start position:19%
the rep and RIR targets. So, here's a
table showing what that might look like

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.640 align:start position:19%
for an intermediate lifter.
Now, you can see in the fourth session,

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:59.680 align:start position:19%
the lifter is able to get 15 reps for
the first set, but they do not increase

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:03.840 align:start position:19%
the load in the next session.
And this is because they had only one

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:07.000 align:start position:19%
rep in a reserve when they did it, not
two.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:10.640 align:start position:19%
So, in the fifth session, they find that
they can do it with two reps in a

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:15.520 align:start position:19%
reserve, and so they increase the load
in the sixth session. So, being very

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:19.000 align:start position:19%
honest with yourself by how many reps
you have left in the tank is absolutely

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:21.720 align:start position:19%
key here.
Now, look, novice, intermediate, and

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:25.680 align:start position:19%
advanced lifters, they're going to
progress at different rates.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:30.120 align:start position:19%
Uh these are just examples, and you're
going to want to adjust according to how

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:34.314 align:start position:19%
you progress. Now, there is one more
crucial part of this, and that is

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:36.560 align:start position:19%
deloads.
&gt;&gt; [music]

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:40.600 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Now, deloads are a recovery tool,
usually taking the form of a short,

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:44.640 align:start position:19%
usually a 1-week period of reduced
volume and or intensity. Now, the goal

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:48.680 align:start position:19%
is to manage fatigue and promote
recovery, so to facilitate long-term

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:54.120 align:start position:19%
progression. Now, we recommend deloads
on an as-needed basis, rather than a

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:59.160 align:start position:19%
pre-planned components of your program.
So, you can think of reactive deloads as

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:03.000 align:start position:19%
an auto-regulated recovery tool.
We recommend using a simple weekly

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:07.120 align:start position:19%
self-assessment to evaluate whether you
need one.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:10.960 align:start position:19%
Answer yes or no to these questions.
So, after completing a week of training,

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:14.919 align:start position:19%
are you dreading the gym? Is sleep worse
than normal? Are your loads or reps the

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:17.544 align:start position:19%
same or decreasing from the last
training cycle, meaning in the last 4 to

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:20.160 align:start position:19%
6 weeks?
&gt;&gt; [music]

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:24.919 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Is stress worse than normal? Are aches
and pains worse than normal? If you can

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:28.440 align:start position:19%
answer yes to none or or maybe one of
these questions, carry on as normal.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:32.600 align:start position:19%
But, if you're answering yes to two or
more of these questions, then you should

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:35.800 align:start position:19%
consider a deload.
Now, there are a number of ways to

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:40.160 align:start position:19%
deload. In general, we recommend
reducing the number of sets that you do

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:44.280 align:start position:19%
by 30 to 50%, while keeping training
frequency and reps in reserve the same.

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:49.000 align:start position:19%
This can be as simple as reducing the
number of sets that you perform for each

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:52.360 align:start position:19%
lift by one or two sets per lift.
You won't always feel equally recovered

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:55.600 align:start position:19%
throughout your body. So, if you feel
aches and pains in one area more than

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:59.840 align:start position:19%
another, feel free to deload the
exercises specific to that area.

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:03.960 align:start position:19%
By using auto-regulated double
progression along with reactive deloads,

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:07.040 align:start position:19%
you should be able to progress for a
long time.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:11.120 align:start position:19%
The rules will take into account the
swings that we can expect in fatigue and

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:15.080 align:start position:19%
performance, meaning that your progress
faster for longer while avoiding injury

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.320 align:start position:19%
and burnout.
All right. Let me know if you have any

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:22.120 align:start position:19%
questions in the comments. You'll find
links to our free programs in the

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:26.080 align:start position:19%
description. And I'll be back with a
further video on breaking training

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.960 align:start position:19%
plateaus when they happen. So, don't
forget to subscribe.

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:31.480 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; [music]
&gt;&gt; And if you'd like help, we offer

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:36.039 align:start position:19%
one-on-one coaching. I've popped a link
for that in the description, also. And

00:08:33.599 --> 00:08:38.560 align:start position:19%
I'd love to see how my team and I can
help.

